[Music]  in our eye on earth series we can share  a bit of good news in the battle against  climate change  cleaner renewable sources of energy such  as wind and solar are generating more  power in the u.s  than coal that's encouraging because  burning coal produces  planet warming emissions that make sea  levels rise and weather more extreme  cbs news senior environmental  correspondent ben tracy visited a school  district in arkansas  that's turning sun rays into paydays  let's review real quickly jeannie repke  has been a teacher in arkansas  for 24 years why are you a teacher oh my  gosh the students  absolutely completely the students can i  assume you don't do this for the money  if you're in it for the money it's the  wrong profession  you know it's not the right choice rebke  school district in batesville arkansas  prides itself on putting students first  but when it came to paying its teachers  batesville was next to last in this part  of the state  salaries averaged about 45 thousand  dollars  we weren't keeping people because of  that michael hester is batesville's  superintendent  he was losing teachers and having a hard  time getting new ones to move to this  rural arkansas town  of about 10 thousand people people  aren't in this business obviously for  the money but they should not have a vow  to poverty  to teach either thank you jeanne repke  has been working  five nights a week at the local  community center just to make ends meet  but then she started hearing a rumor oh  yeah teachers love to talk  about an unusual solution to  batesville's budget problems  what is that about really what's that  about that would allow this school  district  to truly live up to its name don't sleep  on arkansas  we'll surprise you every time the school  took an unused field outback  and filled it with hundreds of solar  panels  it also put up a new solar canopy  stretching across the entire front of  the high school  in all nearly 1500 panels aimed at  recharging  batesville's budget the batesville is  the pioneers  and they really did pioneer solar in  arkansas  rick vance works for the local energy  company that helped the district save  more than 600  thousand dollars in utility costs they  did it at a time when no one else was  doing it  when now everybody's doing it solar  power costs a lot less than it used to  mainly because it's now cheaper to make  the panels in the past decade  the price of solar has dropped 89  percent  so to both save money and the planet  more than seven thousand schools across  the country are now using solar power  that's up 81 percent in just five years  but as far as anyone can tell batesville  is the only school district  that's turned panels into paychecks  batesville has  reduced the checks they write to  utilities and increase the checks they  write to teachers  with the money it saved and made by  selling electricity back to the grid  batesville has handed out bonuses two  years in a row  boosting every teacher's salary by as  much as fifteen  thousand dollars the district once one  of the worst  is now the best paying in the county are  you getting more resumes these days from  teachers who want to come work here  not only are we getting more resumes  we're getting fewer resignations  that's good jeanne repke has seen her  pay go up by  thousands of dollars enough to dig out  of debt and cut  way back on her hours at the community  center thank you  did you ever in your life imagine you'd  get a raise because of solar panels  no that would not have been one of the  things that i thought but  what a great idea the sun's going to be  shining anyway so why not cash in on  that  and she's grateful her school realizes  that putting students first  starts with their teachers it's good to  know that they  care about us feels really really good  now our teacher jeannie says one of the  best parts about not having to work all  those hours at her second job is she has  more time to help out her students  who are learning from home because of  covid and you know schools across the  country are using their solar panels in  different ways here in washington dc  this school put their solar installation  above their parking lot  anthony you get a little solar shade  here when you park your car all right i  like everything about this story ben  thank you very much  more pay for teachers solar panels and  we like teacher jeannie  we do yeah i wish she was one of my  teachers yeah jump off this put a solar  panel in front of her you'll pick it up  yeah i like her yeah she's great that  was great ben

